  "slug": "elegans-lifespan-0bcf",
  "summary": "Five preprints link vesicle trafficking, autophagy and glycan modification to Aβ and α-synuclein pathology, with C. elegans used in two of them.",
  "synthesis": "The current items center on modifiers of protein aggregation and toxicity in neurodegeneration models rather than on lifespan regulation itself. In C. elegans, SV2C protected against MPP+ and paraquat lethality and partly rescued α-synuclein toxicity without restoring motor behavior, while CALM suppressed Aβ aggregation in worms and in vitro. In mice, neuronal CALM loss accelerated plaque formation, neuronal heparan sulfate sulfation promoted α-synuclein uptake and slowed glymphatic clearance, and autophagy deficiency in App knock-in neurons reduced plaques yet worsened cognition and lifespan depending on Aβ burden. A53T α-synuclein overexpression produced sex-dependent striatal LC3 and LAMP1 changes. The common thread is that clearance and trafficking pathways have context-dependent effects; the worm work is limited to toxicity and aggregation readouts, and no item tests the lifespan interventions in the earlier synthesis.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
